# el2 -- Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x (X1E80100) booting at EL2, for KVM.
#
# Committed in patches/el2/, not fetched: 0003-0029 are Stephan Gerhold's
# wip/qcom-laptops-6.17-el2 series, whose codelinaro branch is dead (mirror:
# github.com/jglathe/linux_ms_dev_kit), and 0002, 0031, 0032 and the 'local:'
# patches were written locally. There is no manifest upstream to take.
#
# Order is the series order and it matters -- these are git format-patch
# commits with Fixes: edges between them. Provenance: docs/PATCHES.md.

0002-ASoC-qcom-x1e80100-lift-speaker-volume-caps.patch
0003-soc-qcom-smp2p-Kick-after-requesting-interrupt.patch
0004-soc-qcom-smp2p-Ensure-there-is-enough-space-for-outb.patch
0005-soc-qcom-smp2p-Use-length-limited-strncmp-for-compar.patch
0006-soc-qcom-smp2p-Drop-redundant-stack-copies-of-entry-.patch
0007-soc-qcom-smp2p-Take-over-outgoing-SMEM-items-from-bo.patch
0008-remoteproc-core-Allow-restarting-detached-remoteproc.patch
0009-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5-Allow-detecting-detached-state-.patch
0010-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5-Add-qcom_q6v5_attach.patch
0011-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5-Send-SMP2P-stop-signal-in-attac.patch
0012-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5_pas-Attach-running-remoteproc-i.patch
0013-wip-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5_pas-Avoid-using-broken-rese.patch
0014-arm64-dts-qcom-x1-el2-Add-qcom-broken-reset-for-remo.patch
0015-rpmsg-core-Call-announce_destroy-only-after-announce.patch
0016-rpmsg-core-Make-it-easier-to-manually-create-endpoin.patch
0017-net-qrtr-smd-Fix-race-conditions-while-opening-closi.patch
0018-local-arm64-dts-qcom-x1-el2-drop-top-level-remotepro.patch
0020-media-iris-Port-firmware-loading-without-TZ-PAS-from.patch
0021-arm64-dts-qcom-x1-el2-Add-iris-video-firmware-node.patch
0023-wip-firmware-qcom-tzmem-self-owner-SHM-bridge-via-qc.patch
0024-dt-bindings-firmware-qcom-scm-Add-qcom-shm-bridge-vm.patch
0025-local-arm64-dts-qcom-x1-el2-request-self-owner-SHM-b.patch
# Keep armed: disarming it drops apss to zero. docs.local/S2IDLE-DRAIN.md
0027-arm64-dts-qcom-hamoa-add-system-power-domain-SS3-sta.patch
0028-platform-arm64-add-Yoga-Slim-7x-IT8987-EC-driver-DT-.patch
0029-drm-msm-a6xx-Fix-stale-rpmh-votes-after-suspend.patch
0030-local-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5_pas-Disable-recovery-for-.patch

# HDR10 output on the eDP panel. Touches only drm/msm/dp, which nothing else in
# this series or in CachyOS's branches goes near.
0031-drm-msm-dp-Send-HDR-static-metadata-on-eDP.patch

# Half the screen was intermittently tinted because a PCC coefficient write did
# not latch, leaving an enabled colour-correction block with a dead red channel.
# The latching fault is untouched; an identity matrix simply never needed
# programming, which puts it out of reach.
0032-drm-msm-dpu-Do-not-program-PCC-for-an-identity-CTM.patch

# Fix: suspend zeroes both the interconnect vote and the DPU core clock, and
# resume restores neither, so scanout restarts underprovisioned and the mixers
# paint the underflow colour (blue) until userspace's first commit. Measured:
# 4 of 8 resumes underran before, 0 of 7 after.
0034-drm-msm-dpu-Restore-the-interconnect-vote-on-runtime-.patch

# Silences two boot warnings: msm's fbdev vmaps its buffer and draws with the
# sys_* helpers, which warn once each unless FBINFO_VIRTFB is set. The flag was
# just never set; drm_fbdev_dma/shmem, omapdrm, exynos and tegra all set it.
0035-drm-msm-Set-FBINFO_VIRTFB-on-the-fbdev-framebuffer.patch

# --- hibernate at EL2 ------------------------------------------------------
# Cross-boot resume completes with the fourteen patches below plus
# pcie_qcom.hib_force_teardown=1 on the kernel command line. Everything else
# from the hunt is out of the series -- see the dropped block at the end of
# this section; files stay in patches/el2/ and the reasoning is in
# docs.local/HIBERNATE-EL2.md.
#
# Still broken after a resume and NOT addressed here: the eDP panel stays
# dark, and the ADSP glink edge is stale, so battery, thermal, audio and UCSI
# behind it all time out.

# Upstream in v7.3-rc1, absent from the 7.2 base: resume_target_kernel() masks
# only IRQs, so a Debug/SError/FIQ taken during the restore copy -- when no
# exception vectors are mapped -- is fatal and silent. At EL1 the firmware
# hypervisor absorbs SError/FIQ routing; bare-metal EL2 takes them directly.
# 0038 is the same series' error-path companion.
0037-arm64-hibernate-mask-DAIF-before-restoring-hibernate.patch
0038-arm64-hibernate-Restore-DAIF-state-on-error.patch

# Post-restore, the SMMUv3 hardware and the restored kernel disagree about the
# command-queue indices ("CMDQ error ... Illegal command" storms), the queue
# sticks in error state and every later mapping silently never reaches the
# hardware (F_TRANSLATION storm on the WiFi client, ath12k reset loop). Re-run
# the device reset from .restore_noirq to resync. el2-only exposure: the plain
# dtb leaves pcie_smmu to the firmware hypervisor.
0041-local-iommu-arm-smmu-v3-resync-hardware-on-hibernate.patch

# DEBUG, TEMPORARY -- the hibernate-with-Bluetooth-on crash, 2026-09-12.
# Un-shelved from docs.local/shelved-patches/. Hibernating with the controller
# powered writes the image to 100%, reboots, and the restored kernel dies with
# no journal, no console and no pstore (efi-pstore never registers here, see
# HIBERNATE-EL2.md:1165). The following boot reports "PM: Image not found
# (code -22)" because swsusp_check() clears the swap signature as soon as it
# FINDS the image, before reading it -- so that message is what a crashed
# resume looks like, not evidence the image was never written.

# LOAD-BEARING, and default off: needs pcie_qcom.hib_force_teardown=1 on the
# command line, because sysfs only reaches the kernel that WRITES the image,
# where it changes nothing. The kernel that READS the image leaves pcie4's RC
# live at quiesce -- its endpoint is driverless (no ath12k in the initramfs)
# so dw_pcie_suspend_noirq() takes the d3cold early return -- while the
# restored image carries suspended=true and re-inits warm hardware. No single
# register write in that re-init is lethal; the inherited state is. Tear the
# RC down at freeze regardless, so restore re-inits cold hardware, which is
# the state test_resume proves works.
0057-local-PCI-dwc-qcom-force-RC-teardown-at-hibernate-freeze.patch

# Real protocol bug, not hibernate-specific. receive_version_ack() resends
# VERSION whenever the acked feature mask differs from ours, so a remote that
# acks a SUPERSET of what we offered produces a byte-identical resend and an
# unbounded VERSION/VERSION_ACK ping-pong. Seen against the ADSP (we offer
# 0x1, it acks 0x7) at ~4 us per iteration; after 65 ms the doorbell storm
# took the DSP firmware out.
0066-local-rpmsg-glink-no-livelock-on-superset-version-ack.patch

# The GPU restore. Proven together and only together -- 0069 alone took the
# failure count 5 -> 1, 0068 closed the last one. Both engage only if the GPU
# is runtime-ACTIVE at freeze (~/hibernate-script.sh does this); otherwise
# adreno's freeze/restore are 5 us no-ops and the GMU is never brought up in
# the restore path at all.
#   0068  GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP is software state, so it comes back from the
#         image while the RSC comes from a reboot that reset it rather than
#         slept it. a6xx_rpmh_start() then polls for an ACK that cannot
#         arrive: "Unable to power on the GPU RSC". Clear the bit on a cold
#         boot, a decision derived from hardware (CX_MISC_TCM_RET_CNTL), not
#         from the image.
#   0069  the arm-smmu (v2) driver behind the GPU aliases .restore onto
#         .resume, which early-returns while the device is runtime-suspended.
#         "Runtime-suspended" describes the image, not the silicon: the reset
#         is skipped, the hardware never learns the restored stream mappings
#         and the GMU cannot fetch its firmware.
0068-local-drm-msm-a6xx-clear-stale-pdc-sleep-on-cold-boot.patch
0069-local-iommu-arm-smmu-reprogram-on-hibernate-restore.patch


# BACK IN 2026-09-10, one build after being trimmed out -- removing it is what
# proved it load-bearing. The ADSP edge is stale after every cross-boot resume
# and cannot be recovered from Linux, so every consumer behind it (battmgr, the
# thermal zone, audio, UCSI) pays a full 10 s per request, forever. Measured on
# the trimmed kernel: 34 "intent request timed out" in 5 minutes, all on
# 6800000.remoteproc:glink-edge, zero successful requests between them, 28
# battmgr -110s, systemd-journald and upowerd parked in D state and the load
# average climbing 2.9 -> 4.1 with nothing runnable. Three consecutive timeouts
# (30 s of total silence) is enough to call the remote gone and fail fast; any
# success resets the count and a re-registered edge starts from zero.
0072-local-rpmsg-glink-fail-fast-when-the-remote-is-gone.patch


# Fix candidate, UNTESTED, and the last known step in the post-resume GPU
# failure. 0068 gets the first (cold) bring-up right; this is the one after
# it. a6xx_rpmh_stop() sets GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP unconditionally, including
# right after its own poll timed out and it logged "Unable to power off the
# GPU RSC". The next bring-up reads warm from hardware, so 0068's cold-boot
# clear does not apply, and a6xx_rpmh_start() waits for a wake ACK from an
# RSC that never slept -> OOB timeouts -> DPU preemption timeout -> hangcheck,
# and 3d00000.gpu ends at runtime_status=error. Roughly three minutes after
# every cross-boot resume, and it needs a use/idle/use cycle to show.
# Only arm the wake when the sleep was acknowledged. Residual risk in the
# patch header; depends on 0068.
0075-local-drm-msm-a6xx-do-not-claim-RSC-sleep-after-a-failed-power-off.patch
# Keep: user A/B confirmed resume fails without 0076 (2026-09-12).
# 0076 (temp-alarm register dump) shelved 2026-09-15: the "load-bearing"
# claim was a stale A/B from before 0077. Re-tested without it -- four
# hibernate cycles, all eight temp-alarm IRQ counters at 0, no storm. 0077 is
# the whole fix. Full write-up in shelved-patches/README.md.

# THE FIX for the PMIC interrupt storm, found by the 0076 register dump (since
# shelved to docs.local/shelved-patches/). The
# peripheral was never the problem -- status/shdn_ctl1/alarm_ctl come back
# byte-identical and RT_STS is clear. What the reboot resets is the INTERRUPT
# configuration: SET_TYPE and both POLARITY registers go to power-on defaults
# while irq_desc comes back from the image, so Linux runs handle_edge_irq
# against hardware that is now level with no polarity. The ack clears the
# latch, the level input re-latches at once, forever. Those registers are only
# written by qpnpint_irq_set_type() at request time and spmi-pmic-arb has no
# PM ops, so nothing replays them -- which is why unbind/rebind cured it and
# re-initialising the alarm did not. Affects EVERY interrupt behind this
# controller (pmic_glink/UCSI, pwrkey, RTC alarm), not just thermal.
0077-local-spmi-pmic-arb-restore-irq-type-after-hibernation.patch

# The sibling of 0077, one bus over, and the reason keyboard/touchpad/EC/lid
# are all dead after a resume. msm_pinctrl_resume() re-applies only the pin
# mux (ctl register) via pinctrl_force_default(); the interrupt config lives
# in intr_cfg -- polarity, detection type, raw-status enable and the TARGET
# PROCESSOR -- and is written only by msm_gpio_irq_set_type() at request time.
# Measured: physically typing on the laptop for 10s after a resume, twice,
# gave +0 on every TLMM input line. Replays irq_set_irq_type() for every
# enabled gpio in .restore_noirq, and logs intr_cfg before/after so one boot
# shows whether the register really was lost.
0079-local-pinctrl-qcom-restore-irq-config-after-hibernation.patch

# 0079 is inert without this. The x1e80100 TLMM driver never set .pm, so NO
# pinctrl PM callback runs on this SoC -- not even the long-standing
# msm_pinctrl_resume(). Proven the direct way: 0079 logs every interrupt it
# reprograms and printed NOTHING after a resume while input stayed dead. Only
# 5 of 86 qcom pinctrl drivers wire this up; sc7280 is the pattern.
0080-local-pinctrl-qcom-x1e80100-wire-up-pm-ops.patch

# FOURTH instance of the same defect class (after SMMU, PMIC irqchip, TLMM).
# NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT -- see the measured result below; the earlier
# claim that it "explains the whole ADSP failure" is retracted.
# IPCC_REG_RECV_SIGNAL_ENABLE is
# written only by qcom_ipcc_unmask_irq(), and the reboot clears it. Normally
# resume_device_irqs() rewrites it via the unmask -- but qcom_glink_smem
# requests its interrupt with IRQF_NO_SUSPEND, so it is never masked and never
# unmasked, and nothing rewrites the register. Every doorbell the DSP rings
# after a resume is dropped inside IPCC. Sending still works (SEND_ID is a
# stateless write), which is exactly the asymmetry observed: do_cleanup
# reached the ADSP and tore down its audio service, while zero inbound
# interrupts arrived. The DSP was answering all along.
#
# TESTED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-10, cross-boot (hibtest mode=reboot): it FIRES
# and the ADSP is STILL DEAD. "qcom-ipcc 408000.mailbox: re-armed 2 receive
# signal(s)" at 20:31:13, then battmgr -110 at 20:31:18 and "intent request
# timed out 3 times, treating remote as gone" at 20:31:49. The doorbell was
# only half of it: the reboot also RESETS the DSP, so it has no channels
# open while the restored image still holds ten. Keep this patch -- the
# register really is cleared and really does need rewriting -- and fix the
# channel desync in 0082.
0081-local-mailbox-qcom-ipcc-rearm-recv-signals-after-hibernation.patch

# The other half of the ADSP failure, and the one that actually kills the
# edge: a cross-boot resume resets the DSP, so it comes back with no glink
# channels while the image still describes ten open ones. Unfixable after
# the fact -- that is what all four recovery attempts in ADSP-EL2.md were
# hitting, and why a post-resume rproc stop returns -22 (the SMP2P handshake
# comes out of the image, so the remote never acks). Do it from the other
# end instead: rproc_shutdown() before the snapshot, rproc_boot() after.
# The stop/start cycle is MEASURED clean on a running EL1 machine -- 10
# channels -> 0 -> 10, battmgr and UCSI recover. EL1 only in effect: at EL2
# the DSPs are attach-only, never RPROC_RUNNING, so the notifier is inert.
# Known pre-existing limitation it does NOT fix: audio. After any ADSP
# restart the PDR ack for msm/adsp/audio_pd is never sent, the DSP faults
# ~10 s later (servreg_monitor.c:537) taking CDSP with it, both recover, and
# the sound card does not come back. Disable with
# qcom_q6v5_pas.hibernate_restart=0.
0082-local-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5_pas-restart-the-DSP-across-hibernation.patch

# 0082 ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH -- tested 2026-09-11, it fails with -110. The freeze
# half works (both DSPs "stopped before the image"), but rproc_boot() on the way
# back never sees the SMP2P ready signal. Root cause one layer down, MEASURED
# with the qcom_smp2p tracepoints on the failed machine:
#     irq 17 smp2p-adsp (IPCC) +1   <- the ADSP does ring the doorbell
#     irq 202 q6v5 ready       +0   <- no edge is ever raised
#     smp2p_notify_in: slave-kernel: status:0x0 val:0x0
# smp2p caches raw pointers into SMEM (in, out, entry->value) and they come back
# from the image, while the resuming bootloader re-initialised SMEM underneath.
# qcom_smp2p_intr() sees a non-NULL ->in and never re-acquires, so every entry
# reads empty. Both directions: it is also why a post-resume rproc stop gets
# "timeout waiting for shutdown response" and then -EINVAL. FIFTH instance of
# the defect class (SMMU, PMIC irqchip, TLMM, IPCC, smp2p). 0082 depends on
# this one; do not test 0082 without it.
0083-local-soc-qcom-smp2p-reacquire-smem-items-on-hibernate-restore.patch

# The last piece. With 0082+0083 the CDSP comes back and the ADSP still gets
# -110, and the resync log shows why: NOTHING MOVED for the adsp
# (in 235b9885->235b9885), so 0083's stale-pointer premise does not describe
# that edge. The difference is entry->last_value. CDSP's item was not published
# yet, so last_value stayed 0 and the first doorbell was a change -> ready
# fired. ADSP's item was already there (bootloader started it), so
# qcom_smp2p_start_in() latched the PREVIOUS incarnation's asserted ready bit,
# and the instance we authenticated asserting it again was status==0, no edge,
# timeout on a DSP that was already up. Latching is correct for smp2p (zeroing
# would re-deliver fatal/stop as rising edges), so fix the assumption where it
# lives: on timeout consult the smp2p LINE LEVEL, which readl()s the shared
# item and cannot be fooled by last_value. Requires fatal+stop clear so a stale
# ready from a remote that did not return cannot pass as a start.
0084-local-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5-fall-back-to-the-smp2p-line-level.patch

# BLUETOOTH AFTER RESUME. Third TLMM instance, after 0079 (intr_cfg) and 0080
# (wiring .pm up at all): the ctl and io registers are lost too, and nothing
# replays them either. Measured via /sys/kernel/debug/gpio, which is a real
# MMIO read -- gpio116, the WCN7850's BT_EN, reads "out high" cold and
# "in low pull down" (the power-on default) after a resume, so pwrseq's
# gpiod_set_value() writes an io register that reaches no pad and hci0 dies at
# "command 0xfc00 tx timeout". Pins with a second owner hid this: uart14's
# gpio56-59 are re-muxed by the BOOT kernel probing a98000.serial before it
# loads the image, and gpio117/WLAN_EN comes back driven at 16mA, a strength no
# DT here asks for, so the boot firmware asserted it -- which is exactly why
# Wi-Fi survives a resume and Bluetooth does not. Saves ctl+io at
# .freeze/.poweroff, writes them back at .restore_noirq ahead of 0079's replay.
# HARD DEPENDENCY on 0079, not just ordering: both rewrite
# msm_pinctrl_dev_pm_ops, and this patch's diff carries 0079's
# .restore_noirq line as CONTEXT. Dropping or reordering 0079 makes this fail
# to apply, and 0080 is what gives x1e80100 pm_ops at all -- without it neither
# does anything on this SoC. Treat 0079/0080/0085 as one unit when touching any
# of them. Kept as three patches because 0080 is independently upstreamable and
# 0079 still carries the before/after logging that has to stay for now.
0085-local-pinctrl-qcom-restore-gpio-pad-state-after-hibernation.patch

# THE DPU UNDERRUN FIX (0086 + 0091 squashed 2026-09-12). 0034 fixes the
# bandwidth vote for runtime PM but cannot reach the hibernate path --
# SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS points .restore at pm_runtime_force_resume(), which
# no-ops because needs_force_resume comes out of the image (the "returned 0
# after 0 usecs" in the day-one notes). This adds a real .restore that re-votes
# bw then clock, plus dpu_vbif_init_memtypes() and the encoder resume, and a
# bw_debug knob (default off) that logs the calculation's inputs.
#
# RETRACTED 2026-09-11, and the retraction is the load-bearing part. The
# "bw_ctl=0 after resume vs bw_ctl=2441871360 healthy" measurement that drove
# two build cycles was a `tail -6` ARTIFACT: every zero line was timestamped
# inside a ~10 ms window at the END of the capture -- modetest being killed and
# CRTCs 110..114 tearing down at once. The healthy capture's last six lines
# happened to include live ones, the broken capture's did not. bw_debug then
# measured the calculation DURING operation and it is healthy --
# plane_mask=0x1 sum=2034892800, both mode sources agreeing, which also kills
# the stale-crtc->mode theory. DO NOT RESTART THE BANDWIDTH HUNT.
#
# KEEP IT ANYWAY. The missing .restore and the half-zeroed VBIF QoS remapper
# (vbif 0x550-0x5c0, 00300030 -> 00000030, nothing else reprograms it) are
# genuine gaps that this closes. It simply does not fix the panel.
#
# The underruns themselves ARE real: dpu_enc_underrun_cb fires ~2x per frame on
# the eDP encoder, confirmed by tracepoint and visible on an EXTERNAL monitor
# as one correct strip at the top (FIFO prefilled in vblank) then solid blue.
# They are not what blacks the panel (that is 0090) -- INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR is
# 0x000000ff in both states, so a merely-starved eDP would read blue too.
#
# THE CORE CLOCK, folded in from the former 0091 and VERIFIED 2026-09-12. The
# underrun is real and its cause is the DPU core clock at 325 MHz instead of
# 575. Read from silicon at disp_cc + 0x0:
#     healthy  L_VAL=00440059 ALPHA=0000d800 -> 1725.0 MHz
#     resumed  L_VAL=00440032 ALPHA=0000c800 ->  975.0 MHz
# and mdss_mdp_clk_src is pll0/3. Every other PLL register is identical. The
# RCG's own divider is correct on BOTH boots, which is why dumping it proved
# nothing -- it is the PLL feeding it that drifted.
#
# A plain dev_pm_opp_set_rate() cannot fix that: it computes
# forced = (current_rate_single_clk != freq) and _set_opp() returns early when
# !forced && old_opp == opp. Both caches come back in the image, so the call
# programs nothing -- not the clock, not the PLL, not the genpd perf state.
# Step through another OPP first so the cache disagrees; CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT on
# mdss_mdp_clk_src then propagates the real set down into the PLL. Logs
# "hibernate: core clock forced to 534159360 Hz (rc=0)" on a real resume.
0086-local-drm-msm-dpu-restore-bandwidth-and-core-clock-on-hibernate-restore.patch

# THE eDP PANEL FIX, 2026-09-11 20:00. ROOT CAUSE CONFIRMED ON HARDWARE.
# After a cross-boot resume the eDP pixel clock RCG (disp_cc + 0x82a0) has N
# halved -- M=2903 N=7500 instead of N=15000 -- so the clock runs at exactly
# 522,540,000 Hz instead of 261,270,000, i.e. the mode's rate at 1 pixel per
# clock instead of the widebus 2. The INTF timing is widebus-halved on both
# boots (htotal 1512), so the panel is driven at 120 Hz instead of 60. Measured
# two independent ways: modetest -v reported 119.9 vs 59.8 Hz, and the
# dpu_enc_frame_done_cb intervals agreed (8.34 vs 16.72 ms).
#
# 120 Hz is out of range for this 90 Hz panel, so the sink locks the LINK and
# never presents -- which is why link training, DPCD, SMMU, DDR, every DPU and
# DP register and a full mdss_gdsc power cycle all checked out perfectly while
# the screen stayed black. Writing the healthy M/N back by hand and triggering
# CMD_UPDATE made the panel light up: confirmed by repair, not just by reading.
#
# Mechanism: clk_set_rate() is called correctly on every modeset but
# clk_core_set_rate_nolock() bails when the request equals core->rate -- the
# CACHE, restored from the hibernation image. Same class as 0034/0068/0069/
# 0077/0079/0080/0081/0083. Note debugfs clk_rate is NOT a hardware read
# (CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE), which is why an early "78/78 clocks match" sweep
# wrongly killed the clock theory.
#
# STILL OPEN after this: with the rate corrected the panel presents a strip of
# correct pixels then INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR blue -- a real DPU underrun, the
# same thing the external monitor always showed, and a separate bug.
# mdss_mdp_clk_src is NOT its cause (CFG=00000105 src_div=5 on both boots).
0090-local-drm-msm-dp-reprogram-the-pixel-clock-RCG-on-hibernate-restore.patch

# THE GPU FIX. Found with the (now shelved) 0092 instrumentation:
# a6xx_gmu_rpmh_init() programs the
# RSC/PDC sequencer once at probe, and after a cross-boot restore the block is
# reset with nothing reprogramming it. RSC_STATUS0_DRV0 reads 00000000 on the
# first handshake after a resume, where 925 healthy handshakes only ever show
# 01000000 (awake) or 01010006 (asleep). Unprogrammed sequencer -> the sleep
# handshake cannot complete -> GMU OOB timeouts, CP ucode error, hangcheck.
#
# Also DISPROVED the hypothesis going in: the cold boot logs "clearing
# GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP (was clear)", so 0068 is behaving exactly as intended and
# is NOT over-correcting. It stays.
#
# 0093 detects the unprogrammed state from HARDWARE (a zero STATUS0_DRV0) rather
# than any flag -- every flag here comes back from the image and matches by
# construction, which is what hid 0090, 0086 and 0068 -- and reprograms. Needed
# caching the devm PDC mappings so rpmh_init can be re-run without leaking.
0093-local-drm-msm-a6xx-reprogram-the-RSC-sequencer-after-hibernate.patch
#
# VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-12. The reprogram fires and works:
#   53.921256  RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0) -- reprogramming
#   53.921275  cold boot: clearing GMU_STATUS_PDC_SLEEP (was SET)
#   53.921283  rpmh start:entry  STATUS0_DRV0=01000000   <- correct, not 0
# "Unable to power off the GPU RSC" is GONE, and the GPU now runs a fully
# working desktop for ~74 s after a resume (it previously died at the greeter).
# Note "(was SET)": the stale PDC_SLEEP bit really does arrive set from the
# image, so 0068 is doing real work and is NOT over-correcting.
#
# STILL BROKEN, a DIFFERENT fault further along:
#   128.50  hw_init: timeout waiting for GPU to idle
#           status 00800005 irq 00800200 rptr/wptr 12/12   (ring empty, not idle)
#   128.50  gpu fault ring 0 fence 7c / CP ucode error interrupt
#   128.50  hangcheck recover! offending task: Hyprland
#   129.63  *ERROR* cx gdsc didn't collapse      <- recovery then impossible
#   129.64  Timeout waiting for GMU OOB set GPU_SET, forever
#
# Two separable problems. (a) something makes the GPU fault ~74 s in, on a
# hw_init after an idle. (b) a6xx_recover() cannot reset it because the CX
# genpd never collapses -- gpu and gmu both sit runtime_status=active and the
# pd_gate completion times out.
#
# (b) IS NOT A KERNEL BUG -- resolved 2026-09-12. This machine has a local
# /etc/udev/rules.d/99-gpu-runtime-pm.rules that pins the gpu and gmu
# power/control=on whenever it is on external power, which is precisely what
# stops a6xx_recover() collapsing cx. Unplugged, "cx gdsc didn't collapse"
# does not occur at all. Nothing to fix in-tree; it does mean every GPU
# measurement has to be taken on battery or it measures the udev rule.
#
# That leaves (a), which 0094 is the fix candidate for.
#
# a6xx_zap_shader_init() remembers "already loaded" in a function-local static.
# That flag comes back SET out of the hibernation image while the reboot reset
# the secure world, so the shader is not loaded in hardware, hw_init takes the
# early return, and then tells a still-secure GPU to leave secure mode. The
# in-tree comment at the call site predicts exactly this ("a permissions
# violation will soon follow"). Same defect class as 0090/0086/0093/0068/0069:
# one-shot software state that survives the image and matches by construction.
0094-local-drm-msm-a6xx-reload-the-zap-shader-after-hibernate.patch
0095-local-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5_pas-use-boot-lite-IDs-after-hibernate.patch
0096-local-ASoC-qcom-q6apm-enable-responses-before-child-probes.patch

# Not a hibernate bug, despite being found here: a plain "echo stop > .../
# remoteproc0/state; echo start > ..." reproduces it in two seconds on a
# never-hibernated system. Every clock the LPASS macro codecs use comes from
# &q6prmcc, a clock controller on a GPR service of the ADSP, so restarting the
# DSP unregisters the provider under live consumers. clk_unregister() does not
# fail -- it installs clk_nodrv_ops (.prepare returns -ENXIO) -- so the handles
# are dead for good, runtime_resume fails, power.runtime_error latches, and all
# four macros sit in runtime_status "error" with the card present and silent.
# Recording the dependency makes the driver core unbind the codecs BEFORE the
# supplier's remove runs, so they release live clocks and get re-probed when the
# DSP returns. Do not "fix" this by re-probing afterwards -- see shelved 0098,
# which froze the machine twice.
0099-local-ASoC-codecs-lpass-macro-depend-on-the-dsp-that-provides-the-clocks.patch

# The other half of 0099. A device link makes the consumers release their
# clocks cleanly when the DSP goes down, but the link is purged with the
# provider's device (device_del -> device_links_purge), so the replacement
# provider has nothing recording that anyone is waiting for it. The codecs are
# left UNBOUND rather than deferred, deferred probe only retries the deferred
# list, and the card never returns at all. Attach them from the provider's
# probe. Attach-only on purpose -- releasing a driver there would tear the
# stack down over already-unregistered clocks, which is what hung the machine
# with shelved 0098.
0100-local-ASoC-qcom-q6dsp-lpass-clocks-rebind-consumers-when-the-provider-returns.patch

# Fallout from 0099/0100 making the unbind/rebind path reachable at all: these
# drivers' remove paths have never run on this SoC and are broken three ways.
# wsa_macro leaks its clk registration (clk_hw_register, not devm) so re-probe
# dies -EEXIST and the card never returns; nobody calls pm_runtime_disable() so
# the next probe warns "Unbalanced pm_runtime_enable!"; and every remove
# double-disables the clocks its runtime_suspend already dropped, because
# __device_release_driver() runtime-suspends before calling .remove() -- that
# is the WARN storm, and the panic when panic_on_warn is set.
0101-local-ASoC-lpass-macro-soundwire-make-the-remove-paths-work.patch

# The dependency chain is two deep -- q6prmcc -> macro -> SoundWire master --
# and 0100 only covers the first hop. The masters take "iface" from a macro, so
# they are unbound with it and then never rebound (force-unbound devices are not
# on the deferred list), leaving no SoundWire bus, no wsa884x, and a card device
# that stays bound with no ALSA card behind it. Same attach-only trick, applied
# where the macro re-registers its fsgen.
0102-local-ASoC-lpass-macro-rebind-fsgen-consumers-after-a-dsp-restart.patch

# 0102 gets the SoundWire masters re-attached; they then fail to probe, because
# of_irq_get() cannot remap their interrupt. The DT says IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH and
# the driver requests IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING over the top of it; the mapping
# survives the unbind, so the second of_irq_get() hits "type mismatch" and gets
# virq 0 back -- which the driver does not reject, so it request_irq(0)s and
# fails -EINVAL. Dispose the mapping on unbind so the next probe rebuilds it.
0103-local-soundwire-qcom-drop-the-irq-mapping-on-unbind.patch

# Last hop. With everything below it rebinding, the card still does not come
# back: it holds DAI-name pointers borrowed from the components
# (snd_soc_dai_name_get() does not copy), stays bound on ASoC's rebind list
# when they go away, and retries forever against freed names -- "CODEC DAI
# not registered", with the name printed empty. Unbind/bind of "sound" by hand
# fixes it; a managed link with DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER makes the core do
# the same thing at the right moment.
0104-local-ASoC-qcom-common-follow-the-dai-components-the-card-resolved.patch

# The cold-boot delay: apm_probe() asks the DSP for SPF state ~40 ms after the
# ADSP reports up, SPF is not answering yet, and the flat 5 s wait gates
# everything behind it -- macros bind 35 ms after it gives up, card0 70 ms after
# that. Measured: card at 12.33 s instead of ~7.1 s, which is why the first
# speaker-test after a cold boot finds no card. Poll with short waits instead of
# parking on the worst case. Adds a timeout-taking variant of
# audioreach_send_cmd_sync(); the old entry point and every other command are
# unchanged.
0107-local-ASoC-qcom-q6apm-poll-for-spf-readiness-instead-of-one-long-wait.patch

# BLUETOOTH AFTER RESUME, second half (0085 is the first). With the pad restored
# the chip is enabled -- sampling gpio116 every 50 ms through a power-on shows a
# clean low->high at +60 ms on each of the four qca_setup() retries -- and it
# still answers nothing. port->setup is a bool set once in
# qcom_geni_serial_port_setup() and cleared NOWHERE, so after a cross-boot
# resume qcom_geni_serial_startup() skips the only code that programs the serial
# engine: SE firmware load, setup_fifos(), RX_STALE_CNT, packing, watermarks,
# mode select. Confirmed by repair: rebinding hci_uart_qca alone fails
# identically, rebinding a98000.serial (which re-creates the port struct) works
# first try. That also excludes the dev_pm_opp_set_rate() rate cache -- the clk
# core caches against the gcc provider, not the consumer, so it survives an
# unbind and the rebind could not have fixed it. Clears the flag on .restore
# only; suspend/resume keep skipping the work.
0108-local-tty-serial-qcom-geni-re-setup-the-serial-engine-after-hibernate.patch

# Hibernating with Bluetooth ON does not survive the resume; with it OFF it
# always has. The difference is that an open serdev port makes uart_resume_port()
# run the full re-init instead of taking its early return. This closes the port
# and powers the chip down at .freeze so the image records the case that works,
# and brings it back with hci_reset_dev() at .restore. AVOIDS the fault rather
# than fixing it -- any other geni UART open across a hibernate still hits it.
# Brought back from a PM notifier on PM_POST_HIBERNATION, not from .restore:
# the restore callback runs inside dpm_resume(), so a firmware download there
# lands on top of the display and GPU restore and the desktop waits for it.
# The notifier point is after dpm_resume_end() and thaw_processes().
# qca_freeze() also clears hdev->hw_error AND hdev->reset -- both answer "no
# reply" by talking to the chip anyway, and hci_cmd_timeout() -> qca_reset()
# crashes the SoC for a dump, then calls hci_reset_dev() to go round again.
# The crumb instrument that was chasing the underlying fault was withdrawn
# after it turned out to be writing PMIC bytes outside the range 0043
# validated; see docs.local/METHOD.md.
0114-local-Bluetooth-hci_qca-close-the-controller-before-the-hibernate-snapshot.patch

# Twenty of the twenty-eight seconds between 0114's restore callback and a
# working controller are two HCI_INIT_TIMEOUTs caused by the core running
# hdev->shutdown against the chip qca_freeze() already powered off. This skips
# the pre-shutdown VSC when QCA_BT_OFF is set. Reverted once as 0114 rev2, when
# the tree still had the hdev->flush use-after-free that rev4 fixed; retrying on
# the theory that the wait was hiding that bug rather than doing work.
0115-local-Bluetooth-hci_qca-skip-the-pre-shutdown-command-when-the-chip-is-off.patch

# THE WARNINGS LEFT BEHIND BY A DSP STOP/START. Reproduced without hibernating:
# "echo stop > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state; sleep 2; echo start > ...".
#
# 0116 WAS HERE AND IS SHELVED -- it broke the speakers. It linked
# 6e80000.pinctrl to the clock controller so pm_clk would release
# LPASS_HW_MACRO/DCODEC before clk_unregister() retired them, which did remove
# two clk.c:4482 WARN_ON_ONCE splats. But unbinding the pin controller
# force-unbinds its whole gpio subtree, and the reset.gpio.* AUXILIARY devices
# behind wsa884x's reset-gpios never come back -- force-unbound devices are not
# on the deferred-probe list. wsa884x takes that reset with
# devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(), so it probes happily with
# sd_reset == NULL and never deasserts, leaving the WSA8845s in the reset
# spkr_01_sd_n_active's "output-low" put them in. Everything reads BOUND,
# nothing defers, nothing errors, and the speakers are simply UNATTACHED.
# See docs.local/shelved-patches/README.md before reviving it.

# ucsi_unregister() disables notifications as its first act, and PDR calls it
# precisely because the PD carrying that interface has gone -- so the write goes
# to a dead remote and is reported as "failed to send UCSI write request: -5".
# Nothing is wrong and the caller ignores the return. Refuse from pd_running,
# which PDR has already set, instead of learning it from the transport.
0117-local-usb-typec-ucsi-glink-do-not-send-to-a-protection-domain-that-is-down.patch

# "Handover signaled, but it already happened" is SMP2P behaving normally:
# qcom_smp2p_check_ssr() zeroes entry->last_value on every scan once SSR-ack is
# negotiated, so the doorbell carrying the remote's stop-ack re-delivers ready
# and handover with it. The guard is right, the error level is not. NOT fixed in
# SMP2P -- that re-delivery is what raises the ready edge after a restart, and
# making the reset conditional would risk every start falling back to 0084's
# line level and paying a 5 s timeout.
0118-local-remoteproc-qcom_q6v5-a-repeated-handover-signal-is-not-an-error.patch

# Two more the SSR loop surfaced once 0116-0118 stopped hiding the rest of the
# cycle. Neither is new, both are upstream and both predate this stack; they
# only became visible because the restart path now runs to completion.
#
# 0119: ucsi_debugfs_register() is called from ucsi_register() but released
# only from ucsi_destroy(), which pairs up for an interface registered once.
# ucsi_glink registers and unregisters on every PDR down/up, so the second
# registration hits "debugfs: 'pmic_glink.ucsi.0' already exists in 'ucsi'" and
# overwrites ucsi->debugfs, leaking the old struct and its dentries each time.
0119-local-usb-typec-ucsi-drop-debugfs-on-unregister-not-only-on-destroy.patch

# 0120: "Driver needs fixing: Unsorted reg_defaults". Both macro drivers build
# their table at probe by memcpy'ing a codec-version table onto the base one,
# and the version registers interleave with the base range, so the result is
# unsorted even though each half is in order. regcache_sort_defaults() is
# exported for this; same sort(), same comparator, same buffer regmap would
# have sorted itself, so the array is identical and only the pass and the
# message go.
0120-local-ASoC-lpass-macro-sort-the-reg_defaults-we-assemble.patch

# The one real defect of the set, and the second attempt at it. 6e80000.pinctrl
# takes LPASS_HW_MACRO/DCODEC from &q6prmcc through pm_clk and still holds them
# when a DSP restart unregisters the provider, so clk_unregister() retires the
# handles into clk_nodrv_ops: two "unregistering prepared clock", two
# clk.c:4482 WARN_ON_ONCE on the next autosuspend, and a dead LPASS vote for
# the rest of the boot (-ENXIO out of __pm_clk_enable on every later resume).
# 0116 fixed it with a device link and broke the speakers -- see
# docs.local/shelved-patches/README.md. This releases the clocks from a bus
# notifier on the provider instead: BUS_NOTIFY_UNBIND_DRIVER arrives before
# device_remove(), so pm_clk_destroy() unwinds them while they are still live,
# and BUS_NOTIFY_BOUND_DRIVER gets fresh ones. NO BIND STATE CHANGES, which is
# the whole point -- that is what 0116 got wrong.
0121-local-pinctrl-qcom-lpass-lpi-hand-the-clocks-back-before-the-provider-goes.patch

# Found by cycling twice in one boot, which nothing before this stack could do
# -- audio was dead after the first cycle, so a second was meaningless.
# qcom_swrm_probe() requested its interrupt before sdw_bus_master_add() filled
# bus->slaves from the DT, and qcom_swrm_get_device_status() registers any
# attach it cannot find there under a NULL fwnode. On a re-probe the speakers
# are still enumerated on the wire, so the interrupt fires into an empty list,
# claims the DT entry's name, and sdw_of_find_slaves() dies -EEXIST: no card,
# and a driverless zombie device left behind. Request the irq after the bus is
# populated, and disable it before sdw_bus_master_delete() empties the list on
# the way out.
0122-local-soundwire-qcom-do-not-enumerate-before-the-bus-knows-its-slaves.patch

# HW video decode after a cross-boot resume. The iris firmware lives in the
# no-map video_mem carveout, which has no struct page and so is never in the
# hibernation image -- but core->state comes back from the image saying the
# firmware is loaded and booted. iris_pm_resume() then takes the power-collapse
# exit path, re-boots firmware that is not there, and returns -EBUSY; and
# iris_core_init() short-circuits on that same IRIS_CORE_INIT, so nothing ever
# reloads it and every open() of /dev/video0 fails -EBUSY for the rest of the
# boot. Reset the core to DEINIT on .restore. Software side only: the block is
# powered down by the preceding .freeze and its registers must not be touched,
# which is why iris_fw_unload() (it resets the XTSS first) is not reused.
# Textbook METHOD.md shape A, same family as 0094's zap shader.
0123-local-media-iris-reload-the-firmware-after-hibernate.patch
# EL2 CROSS-BOOT RESUME. VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-15: eight consecutive
# hibernate cycles, four on battery and four on AC (GPU power/control=on, so
# both restore paths), with both DSPs attached, card0 present, all five LPASS
# macros rebound and all five q6prm device links re-established afterwards.
#
# The defect, found with SDAM instrumentation now shelved: removing the edge on
# the restore side removes the audio rpmsg child, apr_remove() joins the PDR
# notifier worker, and that worker unregisters q6prm -- whose five 0099 links go
# to the LPASS macro codecs. device_release_driver_internal() on one of those
# codecs never returns, because the DSP the bootloader just started makes their
# clocks unreachable. 0125 moves that teardown to hibernate entry, where the DSP
# is still ours, and keeps the remaining register half off the sleep lock.
# It is 0125+0129+0142+0144 squashed 2026-09-15, verified byte-identical to the
# tree those four produced, so the hardware result above still stands.
#
# Needs SYSTEMD_SLEEP_FREEZE_USER_SESSIONS=0 (permanent drop-in under
# /etc/systemd/system/systemd-hibernate.service.d/): the entry teardown waits on
# snd_card_disconnect_sync(), which needs userspace able to close its ALSA files.
0125-local-remoteproc-qcom-pas-rebuild-the-attach-only-GLINK-edge-across-hibernate.patch
0126-local-soc-qcom-pdr-drain-workers-before-freeing-services.patch
0127-local-soc-qcom-apr-synchronize-service-callback-removal.patch
0135-local-fastrpc-retain-coherent-DMA-handle-in-buffers.patch

# The blue screen, a SEPARATE and still-open issue: "enc42 intf5 ctl 1 reset
# failure: -22" reproduces on a cold boot at the first kickoff after
# dpu_kms_hw_init, and on resume in the same place. CTL_SW_RESET bit 0 is stuck,
# so no vblank ever arrives and drm_fb_helper_damage_work spins. Logs the raw
# word at ctl init (silent when clear) to tell a stale handover from a reset
# Linux failed to finish, and writes the idle value back at the point of
# failure so we learn whether that alone brings the panel back. Has not fired
# since it was added -- the bit was clear at init on every boot so far.
0143-local-drm-msm-dpu-recover-a-ctl-left-in-software-reset.patch

#
# VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-12, on BOTH power states:
#   38.668993  qcom_scm: PM: platform_pm_restore returned 0   <- SCM back first
#   38.697375  adreno 3d00000.gpu: hibernate: zap shader will be reloaded
#   45.193333  RSC sequencer unprogrammed (STATUS0_DRV0=0) -- reprogramming
#   45.193360  rpmh start:entry  STATUS0_DRV0=01000000
# then nothing: zero CP ucode error, zero hw_init timeout, zero gpu fault, zero
# hangcheck, zero "cx gdsc didn't collapse". devfreq logged 1850 transitions
# reaching the top 1250 MHz OPP, so the GPU is genuinely rendering rather than
# merely quiet. THE DESKTOP SURVIVES A CROSS-BOOT RESUME AND STAYS USABLE.
#
# Both power states had to be tested: they take different restore paths, thanks
# to the local udev rule that pins the GPU runtime-active on external power.
# power/control=on is pm_runtime_forbid(), which holds a usage_count, so
# pm_runtime_need_not_resume() (needs usage_count <= 1) is false and
# pm_runtime_force_suspend() sets needs_force_resume instead of marking the
# device suspended -- the GMU is then booted EAGERLY inside the restore and zero
# rpmh handshakes follow. On battery it stays suspended across the restore and
# the GMU comes up lazily at the first submit, then cycles normally. Clearing
# the flag in .restore is right for both: it runs before adreno_system_resume()
# either way, and hw_init() -- the only caller of a6xx_zap_shader_init() -- is
# reached from msm_gpu_submit() regardless.
#
# This was the LAST fault in the resume path. 0090 + 0086 + 0093 + 0094 together
# take this machine from "black panel" to a working desktop after hibernate.
#
# VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-09-11 20:20. Read from silicon across four boots:
#   healthy N=15000 | broken N=7500 | after manual poke N=15000
#   after 0090, automatically on a real resume: N=15000
# and the restore logs "pixel clk cache 261270000 -> 130635000 Hz". THE PANEL
# NOW PRESENTS AFTER A RESUME instead of showing nothing.
#
# What it presents is INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR blue -- the second, independent
# fault. Note every earlier underrun measurement was taken while the clock ran
# at 2x (double the fetch demand), so none of them distinguish an independent
# bug from a consequence of this one. Next suspect: disp_cc_pll0 at
# disp_cc + 0x0, parent of mdss_mdp_clk_src (src_sel=1, src_div=5 => pll0/3).
#
# RESULT 2026-09-11 19:30: the MDSS wrapper is NOT the cause. 0x090..0x0CC
# looked like firmware state lost across hibernate (1 healthy / 0 resumed) but
# it is an ACTIVITY flag: healthy reads 0 when idle, and the resumed machine
# reads 1 on all sixteen when modetest holds a mode. Writing 1 sticks and
# changes nothing -- no LM CRC, panel still black. The dump only does
# pm_runtime_resume_and_get(), which powers the block but starts nothing.
#
# KEEP IT: read-only, and the only view of ae00000 that exists. But fix it to
# hold a mode before trusting any future comparison.
#
# RESULT 2026-09-11 18:45: the SMMU is NOT the cause. The restore print says
# "106 of 117 stream mapping groups disagree", which looks damning next to the
# GPU SMMU's 0 of 24 -- but the debugfs dump shows every disagreement is
# qcom_smmu_write_s2cr()'s bypass_quirk substitution (89 FAULT->FAULT cb0/cb255,
# 5 BYPASS->TRANS cb255->cb73=bypass_cbndx). ZERO groups that should translate
# fail to. MDSS SID 1c00/0002 is TRANS on cb 8; cb 8 has cached TTBR0 == live
# TTBR0 and SCTLR M=1. The GPU reads 0 only because qcom_adreno_smmu_500_impl
# does not install .write_s2cr.
#
# KEEP IT for now anyway: it is read-only, it is the only view of cached-vs-live
# SMMU state on this machine, and the count in the restore log is a cheap
# regression check. If it is ever re-read, teach it about bypass_quirk first so
# the count stops crying wolf.

# 0086 SECOND PREMISE ALSO RETRACTED, 2026-09-11 late. The VBIF claim -- "half
# the QoS remapper at 0x550-0x5c0 comes back zeroed, 00300030 -> 00000030" --
# came from dpu-HEALTHY-NOSDDM.txt vs dpu-BROKEN-NOSDDM.txt, and that pair is
# NOT comparable. sspp_8 SSPP_FETCH_CONFIG reads 00000087 healthy and 000c0087
# resumed; _dpu_hw_sspp_setup_format() writes the highest_bank_bit field ONLY
# for non-linear formats, so one side was fetching LINEAR and the other UBWC.
# The healthy side also had two planes active (sspp_9, a 512x512 leftover) to
# the resumed side's one, and dpu_vbif_set_qos_remap() runs PER ACTIVE PLANE --
# which is exactly why the UPPER 16 bits of all twelve remap registers differ.
#
# So the VBIF re-init may still be correct; its EVIDENCE was never valid. Do not
# cite those twelve registers again without a controlled capture from
# ~/clean-dump.sh, which pins format, plane count and mode on both sides.

# THE PANEL'S OWN POWER SEQUENCE: raised and DISPROVEN ON HARDWARE the same
# evening, 2026-09-11. Written up as 0087, now in docs.local/shelved-patches/.
#
# The theory: this is a Samsung ATNA33XC20 OLED whose driver warns that "once
# EL_ON3 drops we absolutely need a power cycle before the next enable or the
# backlight will never come on again", and which enforces T12 (500 ms min off)
# in software against p->powered_off_time -- a stamp that comes out of the
# SNAPSHOT IMAGE on a cross-boot restore, i.e. the wrong epoch, so the wait is
# skipped and the panel is re-powered too soon.
#
# The measurement that killed it. On the resumed, black machine, with sddm
# stopped so fbcon owned the CRTC, fb0/blank drove a full FB_BLANK_POWERDOWN ->
# drm_client_modeset_dpms(OFF) cycle straight through the panel driver:
#
#     1.09s  active     -> suspending
#     1.15s  suspending -> suspended     <- regulator off, powered_off_time
#                                           stamped correctly, from THIS boot
#     4.23s  suspended  -> resuming      <- 3082 ms genuinely powered down
#     4.34s  resuming   -> active        <- 110 ms, T12 already satisfied
#
# That is a real, spec-honouring power cycle -- six times the T12 minimum, rail
# down, EL_ON3 down, full prepare/enable chain and link retrain on the way back.
# THE PANEL STAYED BLACK. So the panel's power sequence, its power-on timing and
# the DDIC's post-power-cycle state are all excluded, and 0087 fixes nothing.
#
# Worth keeping for the exclusion, which is a large one: combined with the
# byte-identical DPCD, the whole "the panel is latched off" branch is now dead.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADSP: four RECOVERY approaches exhausted 2026-09-10. NOT closed -- see the
# correction at the end of this block.
#
# After a cross-boot resume the ADSP answers NOTHING. Measured with the
# qcom_glink tracepoints on a resumed machine: one VERSION sent (0078's knob),
# and our own tx is the ONLY glink event in the whole capture. No reply, no
# late arrival. It has not crashed either -- the smp2p `fatal`, `ready`,
# `handover` and `stop` interrupts are all at zero and remoteproc still reads
# `attached`. It is alive and simply does not respond.
#
#   rproc detach/attach (0065)  mechanism works, the DSP then crashes
#   glink edge cycle    (0067)  oopsed, NULL deref
#   GLINK SSR do_cleanup(0073)  no CLEANUP_DONE; destroys a healthy edge
#   fresh VERSION       (0078)  total silence
#
# The one thing that would work -- restarting the DSP -- needs TZ/PAS, and at
# EL2 qcom_pas_ops_no_reset has no .start and no .load. That is the whole
# reason 0008-0014 exist to attach to an already-running DSP.
#
# Consequence: battery, charging, thermal reporting and audio stay dead after
# a cross-boot resume, and 0072 is what keeps that from taking the machine
# down with it. Do not retry those four as-is.
#
# CORRECTION: "wall, stop" was an overclaim. 0013's commit message says PAS
# AUTHENTICATION WORKS at EL2 -- only the hardware reset register sequence is
# missing, and on this firmware split that sequence is performed by the QC
# hypervisor. That is why Windows can restart the DSP on resume and we cannot:
# we removed the hypervisor by booting at EL2. It is a reverse-engineering
# gap, not a security boundary.
#
# Also unexplained: the failure is ONE-DIRECTIONAL. DSP -> Linux works after a
# resume (irq 228 glink-smem keeps counting; the DSP sent GPR packets during
# the do_cleanup test). Only Linux -> DSP is ignored. And SMEM, where the
# glink FIFOs live, is no-map reserved memory -- not in the image, and
# re-initialised by the bootloader every boot.
#
# A hibernate that resumes into an unusable machine is not a working feature.
# Either this gets fixed or hibernate is not shippable.
#
# NOT ruled out for later: at EL1 the DSPs are kernel-loaded via TZ/PAS
# (state=running), so a plain `rproc stop` + `start` should genuinely restart
# the ADSP after a resume. That is an EL1-only recovery and useless for a KVM
# setup, but it has never been tried and would confirm the diagnosis.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0073/0074 DROPPED AGAIN 2026-09-10 -- the do_cleanup route is dead.
#
# Re-tested on a HEALTHY boot with the whole qcom_glink tracepoint group armed
# and a 3 s baseline proving tracing was live. After do_cleanup("apss"):
# ZERO version/open/open_ack/close/close_ack events in either direction, only
# PMIC_RTR_ADSP_APPS data, then "timeout waiting for cleanup done message",
# then 3 intent timeouts and a dead edge. The DSP does receive it (18x
# "qcom,apr GPR: Port not registered" = its audio service tearing down) but it
# never restarts negotiation.
#
# So do_cleanup DESTROYS a healthy edge rather than recovering one, and must
# never be wired into the restore path. The earlier note claiming it made the
# ADSP re-announce all nine channels is RETRACTED; 0067 (edge cycle knob, later
# dropped for oopsing) was in that build and may be what actually produced the
# re-announce.
#
# 0074's premise -- that the remote re-announces after a restart -- has still
# never been observed. It is not disproven, it is untriggered.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0071 DISPROVEN 2026-09-10, two revisions, kept only as a record.
#
# rev1 failed -EINVAL x8 ("invalid TEMP_DAC trip = 3"): qpnp_tm_init() is not
# idempotent, configure_trip_temps() uses chip->ntrips as the hardware stage
# index and probe leaves it at STAGE_COUNT. rev2 rewound ntrips, the restore
# then returned 0 with no errors -- AND THE STORM GOT WORSE: 4064/s per line
# and cpu0 at 92.2% hardirq, against 2371/s and 88.9% with no patch at all.
# So re-running the driver's init is not the fix, and writing
# ALARM_CTRL_FORCE_ENABLE back makes the alarm assert harder.
#
# What unbind/rebind does and .restore does not is RE-REQUEST THE IRQ.
# spmi-pmic-arb has no PM ops whatsoever, and the PMIC-side per-interrupt
# registers are written only at request/activate/unmask/set_type time:
# qpnpint_irq_domain_activate() does EN_CLR + LATCHED_CLR, irq_unmask does
# EN_SET, irq_set_type does SET_TYPE. All of that happens once, at probe.
# That is the leading hypothesis now, one layer below the thermal driver.
#
# NOT yet a patch: three hypotheses about this storm have now been wrong, and
# qpnpint_irq_unmask() only rewrites hardware when EN_SET reads back clear, so
# "replay the irq setup" may be a no-op too. The next step is EVIDENCE -- read
# the temp-alarm peripheral's SPMI registers on a storming machine and on a
# healthy one and diff. PMIC SPMI reads are known safe here (the pinctrl
# pinconf-groups read of REG_EN_CTL worked fine).
#
# WORKAROUND that does work, no reboot: ~/tmfix.sh (cycles the driver binding
# on all eight alarms, which re-requests every irq).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRIMMED 2026-09-10 -- everything that does not contribute to the resume
# completing. Six went out; 0072 came straight back (see above), so five are
# gone. Each reverses cleanly on its own against the full tree, so any one goes
# back by re-adding its line here AND to patches/series.generated (the PKGBUILD
# reads the generated file).
#
#   0039  SCM waitq wait bounded to 30 s, plus IRQF_NO_SUSPEND on the SCM irq.
#         Re-enabled 2026-09-12 for the 0095 resume freeze and shelved again
#         the same day: it never fired, so it measured nothing and remains an
#         unproven change to a path every SCM caller shares.
#   0040  DPM_WATCHDOG around device_prepare(). CONFIG_DPM_WATCHDOG is not set
#         in this build, so it compiles to nothing; it was only ever live with
#         a debug fragment that no longer exists.
#   0058  ath12k unbind across hibernation (default off). Superseded by
#         measurement: the restore now completes with WiFi loaded, so the
#         "must unload ath12k" rule this automates is obsolete.
#   0073  glink_ssr do_cleanup debug knob. Its experiment is finished:
#         do_cleanup("apss") does make the ADSP re-announce all nine channels.
#         Needed again only to resume that work, and only with 0074.
#   0071  PMIC temp-alarm re-init on restore. DISPROVEN ON HARDWARE and it
#         made things WORSE -- see the block below.
#   0074  drop stale channels when the remote restarts its link -- the Linux
#         half of the 0073 result. The most invasive change written in this
#         hunt (it frees channels and unregisters rpmsg devices from the RX
#         path) and it has never actually executed.
#
# Earlier passes, same rule, files kept in patches/el2/ or
# docs.local/shelved-patches/: 0042, 0046, 0048-0056 (fix candidates and
# restore skip-switches), 0043/0044/0045/0047/0059/0060/0062/0063/0064 (SDAM
# crumb instrumentation -- channel exhausted), 0061 (ramoops: DRAM does not
# survive a reset on this box), 0065 (rproc .detach -- mechanism works, fix
# does not), 0067 (glink edge cycle knob -- OOPSED), 0070 (printk inside the
# KMS teardown -- HUNG hibernate ENTRY), 0071 (spmi temp alarm reprogram --
# no effect).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vendored from armada (originally ARMADA_REF eb63d011a3a9), keeping
# USE_ARMADA=no. Generic fixes: 0204 stops tsens lower-threshold irqs aborting
# s2idle. 0517 now retains only the mux/switch array initialization missing
# from cachyos-7.2.5-1; the base already handles EPROBE_DEFER correctly.
# Rebase notes: docs/PATCHES.md.
0204-thermal-qcom-tsens-mask-lower-threshold-irqs-across-suspend.patch
0517-usb-typec-mux-dont-swallow-EPROBE_DEFER.patch

# Local. Stops timerfd_resume() waking a kworker on every s2idle entry, which
# spun the freeze/unfreeze loop at ~10 kHz. docs.local/S2IDLE-DRAIN.md
0207-local-timerfd-make-the-resume-notification-deferrable.patch

# Gives the board a timed wake at last: an RTC alarm on the mmio QTIMER's
# physical timer, since the PMIC RTC alarm is ADSP-owned. Becomes rtc1;
# alarmtimer binds it, so suspend-then-hibernate becomes available.
# OFF by default -- needs arm_arch_timer_mmio.rtc_alarm=1 on the cmdline.
# sc8280xp has the same timer node, so it must not self-enable.
0209-local-clocksource-arm_arch_timer_mmio-RTC-alarm-on-the-physical-timer.patch
